<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/778">778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Orlando, Rolando, Hruodlandus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roland was a Frankish military leader under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> and military governor of the Breton March who became an epic hero and a principal figure of the literary cycle known as the Matter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His only historical attestation is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vita-karoli-magni">Vita Karoli Magni</a>, which names him among the Frankish rearguard killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Battle of Roncevaux Pass</a> in 778. Later writers embellished his death, first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-french">Old French</a> Chanson de Roland of the 11th century, then in the Italian poems Orlando Innamorato by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-maria-boiardo">Matteo Maria Boiardo</a> and Orlando Furioso by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto">Ludovico Ariosto</a>. In poetry he is associated with his sword Durendal and his oliphant horn.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a>, in Chapter 9 of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vita-karoli-magni">Vita Karoli Magni</a>, calls him Hruodlandus Brittannici limitis praefectus, &quot;prefect of the borders of Brittany&quot;. Roland was evidently the first official appointed to direct Frankish policy in Breton affairs. His successor, Guy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, like him could not extend Frankish rule over Brittany.</p><p>In 778 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> marched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and received the surrender of the towns he met. On the return, high in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, his army was forced by the narrow, wooded terrain to move in a long line, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a> set an ambush. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a> says the Basques had the advantage because of the lightness of their weapons, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were hindered by heavy arms. Eggihard, the overseer of the king's table, Anselm, count of the palace, and Roland died, and the deed could not be avenged because the attackers had dispersed. According to legend Roland was laid to rest in the basilica at Blaye, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Many medieval tales made him a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. The 11th-century Song of Roland gives him the olifant, a signalling horn, and the unbreakable sword Durendal. The Latin Historia Caroli Magni, known as the Pseudo-Turpin Chronicle, adds his battle with a Saracen giant named Ferracutus. Girart de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienne">Vienne</a> tells of his friendship with Olivier and his engagement to Olivier's sister Aude, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> his tales form part of the 13th-century Karlamagnús saga. Dante sees him, as Orlando, in the Heaven of Mars.</p><p>From the 15th century he is a central figure of Italian verse romances, including Pulci's Morgante, Boiardo's Orlando Innamorato and Ariosto's Orlando Furioso. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> he became a symbol of the independence of the growing cities from the local nobility, and the statue before Bremen City Hall (1404) has been on the UNESCO World Heritage list with the hall since 2004. The English expression &quot;to give a Roland for an Oliver&quot; recalls the Chanson de Roland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
